WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.020
Onion routing is the idea that a network request can be wrapped in layers and passed through several relays, with each relay peeling away only the layer it needs.

2
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:15.560
The image is like an onion because each hop sees just enough routing information for its position.

3
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:21.420
The entry relay knows where the user connection came from, but it does not know the final website in an ordinary web request.

4
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.000
The middle relay passes traffic along without being the beginning or the end.

5
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:31.540
The exit relay sends the request out to the normal internet, but it does not know the original user address.

6
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:35.420
This separation is the heart of Tor's privacy model.

7
00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.200
Tor is not asking one trusted company to hide everything for you.

8
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.140
It is trying to prevent any single relay from having the full picture.

9
00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:49.240
A local internet provider may see a connection to Tor, but not the final destination inside the circuit.

10
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:55.020
The destination site may see a request from a Tor exit relay, but not the user's home or office address.

11
00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.960
A relay that sits in the middle has a narrow job and a narrow view.

12
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.160
Circuits are temporary paths built by Tor software.

13
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:09.260
When Tor Browser needs to browse, it selects relays from a public directory and establishes layered encryption for the circuit.

14
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.840
This layered encryption is different from the encryption used by HTTPS websites.

15
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.220
Tor's layers protect routing information between relays.

16
00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:23.000
HTTPS protects content between the browser and the website when the destination supports it.

17
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.860
These two protections can work together, but they solve different problems.

18
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:30.020
A simple example shows the split.

19
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:44.440
If a person reads an HTTPS news site through Tor, the local network can see a Tor connection, the entry relay can see the user's connection to Tor, the exit relay can see traffic headed toward the news site, and the news site can see a Tor exit address.

20
00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:49.420
The HTTPS layer protects the page contents and form data from the exit relay.

21
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:55.120
The Tor layers keep the original address and final destination from being joined by a normal relay.

22
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.480
It is important not to overstate what relays hide.

23
00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:04.940
The entry relay still sees that a connection is coming from you, even if it does not see the destination.

24
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:13.760
The exit relay may see traffic leaving the Tor network toward a website, and if that traffic is not protected by HTTPS, the exit position may expose content.

25
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:20.340
The destination can observe timing, requests, account activity, and any data you submit.

26
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:26.520
Tor reduces the linkability of source and destination; it does not remove all observable behavior.

27
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.160
The mental model also helps explain performance.

28
00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:36.980
A Tor circuit adds distance, encryption work, and shared relay capacity.

29
00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:45.080
Pages may load more slowly than in a direct browser because the traffic is taking an indirect path through volunteer-operated infrastructure.

30
00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:48.460
Slowness is not necessarily a sign that something is broken.

31
00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:55.200
It is often the cost of avoiding a direct connection that gives more information to fewer observers.

32
00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:05.000
A good learner takeaway is to ask, "Who can see what from this position?" The local network sees that Tor is being used, unless an access method changes that appearance.

33
00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.940
The entry relay sees the user connection but not the final site.

34
00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.600
The exit relay sees traffic leaving Tor but not the user's original address.

35
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:19.060
The destination sees the request, the exit relay address, and any website-level identifiers.

36
00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:23.080
Thinking in roles keeps Tor understandable and prevents magical thinking.
